{"type":"cards","design":"S66_QUAD_RULE_CARDS","title":"Database metadata portability and crash recovery","items":[{"title":"Contained AG user logins","text":"Contained Availability Groups store authentication logins and database permissions inside the AG itself, eliminating orphan users when failing over between Region 1 and Region 2 replicas.","tag":"Security ops","icon":"user"},{"title":"Synchronized SQL agent jobs","text":"Contained msdb system databases replicate SQL Server Agent jobs, schedules, and operators automatically across all availability group nodes without custom sync scripts.","tag":"DBA team","icon":"automation"},{"title":"90% faster crash undo","text":"Accelerated Database Recovery (ADR) uses a Persisted Version Store to reduce SQL Server crash recovery and transaction undo phase execution by up to 90% during abrupt failovers.","tag":"DBA team","icon":"rocket"},{"title":"Aggressive log truncation","text":"ADR decouples transaction log truncation from long-running active transactions, preventing uncontained log growth on E: and L: volumes during cross-region replication lag.","tag":"Cloud ops","icon":"growth"}],"notes":"Standard availability groups leave instance-level logins and SQL Agent jobs in the local master and msdb databases, which causes application authentication failures after a regional failover. By deploying Contained Availability Groups alongside Accelerated Database Recovery (ADR), Contoso Health keeps logins and jobs portable while reducing crash recovery and undo execution by up to 90%. ADR also provides aggressive transaction log truncation so long-running clinical queries cannot fill the transaction log disk during failover events.

Sources:
