{"type":"process","design":"S66_STEP_COLUMNS","title":"Operational runbooks for regional failover","lead":"Standardized disaster recovery procedures guarantee recovery times under the target.","items":[{"title":"Local HA detection","tag":"WSFC cluster","text":"Action: Windows Server Failover Clustering detects a primary node failure in Region 1 and automatically transfers the synchronous primary role to the local secondary zone.","icon":"shield-cloud"},{"title":"ILB traffic shift","tag":"Load balancer","text":"Action: the Region 1 internal load balancer health probe fails on the down node and immediately redirects client connections to the newly promoted local primary.","icon":"network-hub"},{"title":"Cross-region DR promotion","tag":"Contoso DBA","text":"Action: during a Region 1 outage, DBAs set Distributed AGs to sync mode if reachable or force failover with data loss in Region 2.","icon":"database-refresh"},{"title":"DNS routing update","tag":"Cloud DNS","text":"Action: automation scripts update the Cloud DNS CNAME record to point the clinical application listener name to the Region 2 internal load balancer IP address.","icon":"globe-circle"},{"title":"Split-brain resolution","tag":"Cloud engineering","text":"Action: before failed Region 1 nodes return online, engineers fence the old primary subnet and demote the former replicas to secondary role to prevent split-brain writes.","icon":"wrench"}],"notes":"Intra-region node failures are handled automatically by Windows Server Failover Clustering and the internal load balancer in Region 1. For a full regional disaster, the runbook guides Contoso Health DBAs through promoting the Region 2 availability group and updating Cloud DNS. Strict network fencing and replica demotion steps prevent split-brain conflicts when Region 1 recovers.

